    <!-- Fake Teaching Session content for the mockups (learner and agent text). -->
    <string name="fake_t_probe_intro">Before we start, a few questions to find where your understanding ends.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_mod_question">What is 17 mod 5?</string>
    <string name="fake_t_mod_thinking">17 is 3 × 5 + 2, so the remainder is 2.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_public_question">When a message is encrypted with someone’s public key, who can decrypt it?</string>
    <string name="fake_t_public_a">Anyone who has the public key</string>
    <string name="fake_t_public_b">Only the holder of the matching private key</string>
    <string name="fake_t_public_c">Nobody, until both sides agree on a password</string>
    <string name="fake_t_public_thinking">I think only the private key undoes it, but I can’t say why the public one can’t.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_recall_question">In your own words: what makes a function “one-way”?</string>
    <string name="fake_t_recall_answer">It is easy to compute, but hard to go back from the result to the input.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_node_modular">Modular arithmetic</string>
    <string name="fake_t_node_oneway">One-way functions</string>
    <string name="fake_t_node_dlog">Discrete logarithm</string>
    <string name="fake_t_node_trapdoor">Trapdoor functions</string>
    <string name="fake_t_node_dh">Diffie–Hellman key exchange</string>
    <string name="fake_t_node_rsa">RSA encryption</string>
    <string name="fake_t_node_goal">Sharing a secret with strangers</string>
    <string name="fake_t_plan_intro">You already know modular arithmetic and what a one-way function is. Here is the path from there to your goal.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_plan_reply">Does this match what you want to understand? Tell me in your own words if something is missing.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_prediction_question">Alice and Bob each keep a secret number and talk only over a public line. Can they end up with the same secret number?</string>
    <string name="fake_t_prediction_yes">Yes, without ever sending their secrets</string>
    <string name="fake_t_prediction_no">No, one of them has to send a secret</string>
    <string name="fake_t_prediction_thinking">Maybe they mix their numbers with something public, like paint colors?</string>
    <string name="fake_t_reasoning">The learner predicted yes and reached for the paint analogy. Start from the exponent rule mod p, then show the exchange as a sequence, and check whether they see why Eve is stuck.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_dh_lead">**Diffie–Hellman** works because exponents can be applied in either order, even mod *p*:</string>
    <string name="fake_t_dh_steps">- Alice picks a secret *a* and sends *A = gᵃ mod p*.\n- Bob picks a secret *b* and sends *B = gᵇ mod p*.\n- Each raises what they receive to their own secret, and both get *gᵃᵇ mod p*.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_dh_eve">Eve sees *g*, *p*, *A* and *B*, but never *a* or *b*.</string>
    <string name="fake_t_check_question">Eve sees A and B. Why can’t she compute the shared secret?</string>
    <string name="fake_t_check_a">A and B are encrypted before they are sent</string>
    <string name="fake_t_check_b">She would need a or b: a discrete logarithm</string>
    <string name="fake_t_check_c">The shared secret is never computed</string>
    <string name="fake_t_check2_question">Which number do Alice and Bob end up sharing?</string>
    <string name="fake_t_check2_a">g^(a+b) mod p</string>
    <string name="fake_t_check2_b">g^(ab) mod p</string>
    <string name="fake_t_check_thinking">If the numbers were small, Eve could just try every exponent.</string>
